export const CP1={title:'The event and the problem-solving mindset',hook:'A rural clinic’s patients keep missing appointments. A HOSA chapter’s membership is shrinking. A school’s students are skipping breakfast. Each problem has many possible causes and many possible solutions—and the best solution is not always the first one that comes to mind. Creative Problem Solving tests whether your team can think clearly, work together and design a creative, realistic solution under pressure.',objectives:['Describe the Creative Problem Solving event, its test plan and scoring.','Explain how the test and presentation combine in the final score.','Describe the mindset of effective problem solvers.','Plan a year of preparation using the official references.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Creative Problem Solving is a **team event for 2–6 members**. **Round One** is a **50-question test in 60 minutes**. The **team’s average test score** qualifies teams for Round Two and is **added to the presentation score** to determine final results; it also breaks ties. Every member’s test score matters.',
'In **Round Two**, teams receive the same **secret problem** related to HOSA, the health community or a specific health issue, sometimes with **data and resources** provided by HOSA. Teams have **15 minutes to prepare** with **two flip-chart sheets** (provided), **markers** and **index cards** (bring your own), then are escorted to another room for a **10-minute presentation**. Only the prepared index cards and flip charts may be brought in; **all members must take an active role**.',
'Do not discuss the secret problem until the event ends—violations are penalized.',
{t:'table',caption:'Event structure',head:['Round','Format','Scoring'],rows:[['One','50-question test, 60 minutes','Team average qualifies and is added to final score'],['Two: prep','15 minutes, 2 flip charts, markers, index cards','Warnings at 5 and 1 minute'],['Two: presentation','10 minutes to judges','100-point rubric'],['Tie','Highest team test average','Decides final rank']]}]],
['The written test plan',[
'The test covers five areas: **Critical and Effective Thinking (26%)**, **Problem Solving Strategies and Skills (24%)**, **Clarification of Problems and Generating Ideas (10%)**, **Examining Constraints and Developing Alternatives (20%)** and **Components of Logic (20%)**.',
'Questions come from four references: **Organizational Behavior** by McShane and Von Glinow (teams, decision making, creativity), **Decision Making and Problem Solving** by John Adair (decision processes), **Solve It!** by Dietmar Sternad (creative problem solving, including “invisible rules”) and **Critical Thinking and Logic Mastery** by Thinknetic (logic and reasoning). Sample questions cite page numbers, so read carefully and take notes.',
'This course covers each test area and links concepts to the Round Two presentation, where the same thinking skills earn points.',
{t:'table',caption:'Written test plan',head:['Area','Weight'],rows:[['Critical and Effective Thinking','26%'],['Problem Solving Strategies and Skills','24%'],['Examining Constraints/Developing Alternatives','20%'],['Components of Logic','20%'],['Clarification of Problems and Generating Ideas','10%']]}]],
['The Round Two rubric',[
'The presentation is scored out of **100 points**. **Content (80)**: **understanding of the problem** (15), an **imaginative and innovative approach** (15), **explanation of the solution**—clear, concise and logical (15), **applying previous knowledge and experience** to the problem (15), a solution that is **financially sound** (10) and **use of the information provided** in the secret topic (10). **Delivery (20)**: voice, stage presence, diction and team participation (5 each).',
'Notice the balance: judges want solutions that are both **creative** and **realistic**. A brilliant idea with no budget logic loses points, and a safe, ordinary idea loses innovation points. The best solutions are fresh, practical and grounded in the information provided.',
'Learn this rubric by heart—it should shape everything your team does in the 15-minute prep.',
match('Rubric item and points',[['Understanding of problem','15'],['Imaginative and innovative approach','15'],['Explanation of solution','15'],['Applies previous knowledge','15'],['Financially sound','10'],['Use of provided information','10']])]],
['The problem-solving mindset',[
'Effective problem solvers are **curious** (they ask questions before answering), **open-minded** (they consider alternatives and other perspectives), **systematic** (they follow a process rather than jumping to solutions), **creative** (they challenge assumptions and combine ideas) and **realistic** (they test ideas against constraints and evidence).',
'They also **separate** idea generation from judgment. Criticizing ideas too early shuts down creativity; never evaluating them leads to impractical solutions. A good process moves from **divergent thinking** (many ideas) to **convergent thinking** (choosing and refining the best).',
'Finally, they **collaborate**: diverse perspectives produce better solutions, especially when team members feel safe sharing unusual ideas.',
sort('Helpful or unhelpful mindset?',['Helpful','Unhelpful'],[['Asking “What is the real problem?” first',0],['Choosing the first idea immediately',1],['Generating many ideas before judging',0],['Criticizing every idea as it is shared',1],['Testing ideas against budget and time',0],['Ignoring information provided in the problem',1]])]],
['A general problem-solving process',[
'Most models share similar steps: **(1) understand and define the problem**; **(2) gather information and identify causes**; **(3) generate possible solutions**; **(4) evaluate options against criteria and constraints**; **(5) choose and plan the solution**; **(6) implement**; and **(7) evaluate results**. John Adair’s classic approach is similar: define the objective, collect relevant information, generate feasible options, make the decision, then implement and evaluate.',
'In Round Two you cannot implement, but you must **present** an implementation plan—who does what, when, at what cost and how success will be measured. Judges reward teams that show the whole process clearly.',
'Practice using the same process on every practice problem so it becomes automatic under time pressure.',
order('General problem-solving process',['Define the problem','Gather information and find causes','Generate possible solutions','Evaluate options against constraints','Choose and plan the solution','Implement','Evaluate results'])]],
['A preparation plan',[
'**Months 1–3:** divide the four references among team members, but have everyone read summaries of all of them; build a glossary of terms (team charter, satisficing, divergent thinking, syllogism). **Months 3–5:** practice test questions weekly and practice the Round Two process with invented problems, timing 15 minutes of prep and 10 minutes of presentation. **Final weeks:** full mock rounds judged with the rubric, plus timed practice tests.',
'Create a bank of **practice problems** on HOSA and health topics: low chapter membership, vaccine hesitancy in a community, food deserts, nurse shortages in rural hospitals, student stress during exams. Each gives your team a chance to rehearse roles and flip-chart design.',
'Track scores and debrief after each practice to improve both speed and quality.',
order('Year-long preparation',['Read and summarize the references','Build a shared glossary','Practice weekly test questions','Run timed practice problems','Judge mock rounds with the rubric','Debrief and refine roles'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('First practice problem',{a:{text:'Your team gets an invented practice problem: “Teen volunteers at a local hospital have dropped by half.” (Scenario invented for practice.) What should you do first?',choices:[['Clarify the problem and look for causes in the information provided','b','Correct. Understand before solving.',true],['Start drawing a flashy solution immediately','b','Jumping to solutions misses causes.',false]]},b:{text:'Your favorite idea is a paid summer program costing $200,000, but the problem mentions a $5,000 budget. What do you do?',choices:[['Adapt it to fit the budget or choose a different option','c','Correct. Financially sound solutions score points.',true],['Present it anyway','c','Judges score financial soundness.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team presents a creative, low-cost peer recruitment and scheduling solution that uses the data provided.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Teams of 2–6; 50-question test (team average qualifies, is added to the final score and breaks ties); secret problem with 15-minute prep (two flip charts, markers, index cards) and a 10-minute presentation; all members participate.','Test plan: critical and effective thinking 26%, problem-solving strategies 24%, constraints and alternatives 20%, logic 20%, clarification and idea generation 10%; references are McShane and Von Glinow, Adair, Sternad and Thinknetic.','Rubric (100): understanding 15, innovation 15, explanation 15, prior knowledge 15, financially sound 10, use of provided information 10, delivery 20; use a systematic process from defining the problem to evaluating results.'],terms:[['Divergent thinking','Generating many possible ideas.'],['Convergent thinking','Narrowing ideas to choose the best one.'],['Secret topic','A problem revealed only at the event that must not be discussed beforehand.']],mcqs:[q('How is the Round One test score used?','The team average qualifies teams and is added to the final score','It is not used','It replaces the presentation','It chooses the topic','Every member counts.'),q('How long is Round Two preparation?','15 minutes','5 minutes','30 minutes','1 hour','With warnings at 5 and 1 minute.'),q('How long is the presentation?','10 minutes','5 minutes','20 minutes','2 minutes','One-minute flash card.'),q('Which area is the largest part of the test?','Critical and Effective Thinking','Components of Logic','Clarification of Problems','Constraints','26%.'),q('How many flip-chart sheets does HOSA provide?','Two','One','Five','None','Plus your markers.'),q('Which rubric item is worth 10 points?','Financially sound solution','Understanding of problem','Innovative approach','Explanation of solution','Budget logic.'),q('In the classic approach to decision-making, what is the third step?','Generate feasible options','Define the objective','Collect relevant information','Implement and evaluate','HOSA sample question (Adair).'),q('Divergent thinking means:','Generating many ideas','Choosing one idea','Criticizing ideas','Ending the discussion','Then converge.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the Creative Problem Solving event format and scoring, and describe the mindset and process your team would use to solve a secret problem.',rubric:[['Format',['2-6','test','50','team average','added','15 minutes','10 minutes','flip chart']],['Rubric',['understanding','innovative','explanation','prior knowledge','financially','information','delivery']],['Mindset',['curious','open-minded','systematic','creative','realistic','divergent','convergent']],['Process',['define','information','causes','generate','evaluate','constraints','plan','evaluate results']]],model:'Creative Problem Solving is a team event for two to six members. In Round One, everyone takes a 50-question test in 60 minutes on critical thinking, problem-solving strategies, clarifying problems and generating ideas, examining constraints and logic; the team’s average score qualifies it for Round Two, is added to the presentation score and breaks ties, so every member must study. In Round Two, all teams receive the same secret problem, sometimes with data from HOSA, and have 15 minutes to prepare using two flip-chart sheets, markers and index cards before giving a 10-minute presentation in which every member speaks. Judges score understanding of the problem, an imaginative and innovative approach, a clear and logical explanation and application of prior knowledge (15 points each), a financially sound solution and use of the provided information (10 points each) and delivery (20 points). Our team would approach the problem with a curious, open-minded and systematic mindset. We would first define the real problem and look for causes in the information provided, then use divergent thinking to generate many ideas without criticizing them, followed by convergent thinking to evaluate options against budget, time and other constraints. We would choose and plan a solution that is both creative and realistic, describe how it would be implemented and funded, connect it to our prior knowledge and experiences and explain how success would be measured.'},sources:[RULES,OS_OB,OS_MGMT]};

export const CP2={title:'Critical and effective thinking',hook:'A news story says a new energy drink improves test scores. A teammate says, “Everyone knows the problem is lack of funding.” A data table shows clinic visits fell after a policy change. Critical thinkers do not accept these at face value—they ask what the evidence is, what assumptions are hidden and what else could explain the facts. Critical and effective thinking is the largest part of the test (26%).',objectives:['Define critical thinking and its elements and standards.','Recognize common cognitive biases that distort thinking.','Distinguish fast, intuitive thinking from slow, deliberate thinking.','Apply critical thinking to problems and evidence.'],sections:[
['What critical thinking is',[
'**Critical thinking** is the disciplined process of actively analyzing, evaluating and improving one’s thinking. The Foundation for Critical Thinking describes it as thinking about your thinking in order to improve it—using reasoning, evidence and clear standards rather than accepting ideas automatically.',
'Critical thinkers **question assumptions**, **seek evidence**, **consider alternative explanations**, **recognize bias** (including their own) and **reach well-supported conclusions**. Critical thinking is not being negative; it is being careful and fair.',
'**Effective thinking** adds practical judgment: focusing on what matters most, managing time and turning analysis into decisions and action.',
"In health care, critical thinking supports safe decisions: a nurse questions an unusual medication order, a public health worker checks whether a data trend is real and a team leader asks whether a proposed fix addresses the true cause. The same habits help in Round Two.",
sort('Critical thinking or not?',['Critical thinking','Not critical thinking'],[['Asking what evidence supports a claim',0],['Accepting a claim because it is popular',1],['Considering other explanations for data',0],['Ignoring information that contradicts your view',1],['Checking the source of a statistic',0],['Deciding before hearing the facts',1]])]],
['Elements of thought and intellectual standards',[
'The Paul–Elder framework identifies **elements of thought** present in all reasoning: **purpose**, **question at issue**, **information**, **interpretation and inference**, **concepts**, **assumptions**, **implications and consequences** and **point of view**. Examining each element helps you understand and improve any argument or plan.',
'It also lists **intellectual standards** for judging thinking: **clarity**, **accuracy**, **precision**, **relevance**, **depth**, **breadth**, **logic**, **significance** and **fairness**. For example, a solution proposal should be clear (understandable), accurate (true), relevant (addresses the problem), deep (handles complexity) and fair (considers stakeholders).',
'Use these standards to check your Round Two solution before you present it.',
flip('Intellectual standards',[['Clarity','Is it understandable?'],['Accuracy','Is it true?'],['Precision','Is it specific enough?'],['Relevance','Does it relate to the problem?'],['Depth','Does it address complexity?'],['Fairness','Does it consider all stakeholders?']])]],
['Fast and slow thinking',[
'Psychologist Daniel Kahneman described two modes of thinking. **System 1** is **fast, automatic and intuitive**—useful for routine decisions but prone to errors and biases. **System 2** is **slow, deliberate and analytical**—better for complex problems but effortful.',
'Good problem solvers recognize when a situation needs System 2: unfamiliar problems, high stakes, conflicting information or strong emotions. Under time pressure in Round Two, teams can rely too heavily on System 1; a simple structured process helps engage System 2.',
'Intuition still has value—experienced professionals often sense problems quickly—but it should be checked with evidence.',
"One practical habit is to pause before agreeing: count to three, then ask one clarifying question. This small delay engages slower, more careful thinking.",
sort('System 1 or System 2?',['System 1 (fast)','System 2 (slow)'],[['Recognizing a friend’s face',0],['Comparing three solutions with a decision matrix',1],['Jumping to the first idea',0],['Calculating a budget',1],['Reacting to a loud noise',0],['Analyzing data for root causes',1]])]],
['Cognitive biases',[
'**Cognitive biases** are systematic errors in thinking. **Confirmation bias**: favoring information that supports what you believe. **Anchoring**: relying too heavily on the first number or idea. **Availability heuristic**: judging likelihood by how easily examples come to mind. **Overconfidence**: overestimating your knowledge. **Sunk cost fallacy**: continuing something because you already invested in it. **Framing effect**: reacting differently to the same information depending on how it is presented.',
'Teams also face **groupthink**—going along with the group to avoid conflict—which can lead to poor decisions. Recognizing biases helps you avoid them in your own thinking and spot them in test questions.',
'A simple defense: ask “What would change my mind?” and “What are we missing?” before deciding.',
"Other common biases include the **halo effect** (one positive trait colors overall judgment), **status quo bias** (preferring things to stay the same) and **hindsight bias** (believing after the fact that an outcome was predictable). Learning names for biases makes them easier to notice.",
match('Bias and example',[['Confirmation bias','Only reading sources that agree with you'],['Anchoring','Basing a budget on the first number mentioned'],['Availability heuristic','Fearing a rare event because it was in the news'],['Sunk cost fallacy','Continuing a failing plan because of time spent'],['Framing effect','“90% survival” sounds better than “10% mortality”'],['Groupthink','Agreeing to avoid conflict']])]],
['Evaluating evidence and claims',[
'When evaluating evidence, ask: **What is the source?** Is it credible and current? **What exactly is claimed?** Is it a fact, an opinion or an inference? **How strong is the evidence?** Is it a single anecdote or a well-designed study? **Correlation or causation?** Two things happening together does not prove one caused the other. **What is missing?** What other data would help?',
'Distinguish **facts** (verifiable statements), **opinions** (beliefs or judgments) and **inferences** (conclusions drawn from facts). In Round Two, judges reward teams that use the provided data accurately and draw reasonable inferences from it.',
'Be cautious with numbers: check the denominator, time period and whether percentages or counts are being compared.',
"Ask whether a sample represents the whole group. A survey of ten volunteers may not reflect a whole school, and a single success story may not predict results elsewhere.",
sort('Fact, opinion or inference?',['Fact','Opinion','Inference'],[['Clinic visits fell from 500 to 400 last year',0],['The clinic’s new hours are terrible',1],['Visits likely fell because evening hours were cut',2],['The program costs $8,000',0],['Our solution is the best idea ever',1],['Students may prefer text reminders based on survey responses',2]])]],
['Metacognition and reflection',[
'**Metacognition** means thinking about your own thinking: planning how to approach a problem, monitoring your progress and evaluating what worked. Before solving, ask: What do I know? What do I need to find out? During: Am I on track? After: What would I do differently?',
'Teams can practice metacognition through **debriefs** after every practice problem: Did we define the problem correctly? Did we consider enough ideas? Did biases influence us? Did we manage time well?',
'Reflection turns practice into improvement and prepares you to explain your reasoning to judges.',
order('Metacognitive cycle',['Plan: What do we know and need?','Monitor: Are we on track?','Adjust: Change approach if needed','Evaluate: What worked?','Reflect: What will we do differently?'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Questioning a claim',{a:{text:'A problem states that a school started a breakfast program and test scores rose. A teammate says, “So breakfast raised scores.” (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the critical-thinking response?',choices:[['Ask whether other changes happened at the same time—correlation is not causation','b','Correct.',true],['Agree immediately','b','Check alternative explanations.',false]]},b:{text:'Your team keeps defending the first idea because you spent five minutes on it. Which bias is this?',choices:[['Sunk cost fallacy','c','Correct.',true],['Availability heuristic','c','It is about prior investment of time.',false]]},c:{text:'You consider other options and choose a stronger solution.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Critical thinking is disciplined analysis and evaluation of thinking—questioning assumptions, seeking evidence, considering alternatives and recognizing bias; effective thinking turns analysis into action.','Paul–Elder elements of thought (purpose, question, information, inference, concepts, assumptions, implications, point of view) and standards (clarity, accuracy, precision, relevance, depth, breadth, logic, significance, fairness); System 1 is fast and intuitive, System 2 slow and deliberate.','Watch for confirmation bias, anchoring, availability, overconfidence, sunk cost, framing and groupthink; distinguish facts, opinions and inferences and correlation from causation; use metacognition and debriefs.'],terms:[['Critical thinking','Disciplined analysis and evaluation of one’s thinking to improve it.'],['Cognitive bias','A systematic error in thinking.'],['Metacognition','Thinking about one’s own thinking.']],mcqs:[q('Relying too heavily on the first number mentioned is:','Anchoring','Framing','Groupthink','Sunk cost','First idea dominates.'),q('Continuing a failing plan because of time already spent is:','Sunk cost fallacy','Confirmation bias','Availability heuristic','Anchoring','Prior investment.'),q('System 2 thinking is:','Slow and deliberate','Fast and automatic','Always biased','Emotional only','Analytical.'),q('Which is an intellectual standard?','Relevance','Popularity','Speed','Volume','Paul–Elder.'),q('Which is an inference?','Visits likely fell because evening hours were cut','Visits fell from 500 to 400','The program costs $8,000','The clinic opened in 2010','Conclusion from facts.'),q('Going along with the group to avoid conflict is:','Groupthink','Brainstorming','Metacognition','Divergent thinking','Team bias.'),q('Correlation means:','Two things occur together','One thing caused another','A fact is false','A bias exists','Not causation.'),q('Metacognition is:','Thinking about your own thinking','A type of logic error','A budget tool','A team charter','Plan, monitor, evaluate.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how critical thinking and awareness of cognitive biases would help your team analyze a secret problem and its data in Round Two.',rubric:[['Critical thinking',['question','assumptions','evidence','alternative','standards','clarity','accuracy']],['Biases',['confirmation','anchoring','sunk cost','groupthink','availability','framing']],['Evidence',['fact','opinion','inference','correlation','causation','source','data']],['Application',['system 2','process','debrief','metacognition','solution']]],model:'Critical thinking would help our team avoid jumping to conclusions under time pressure. When we receive the secret problem, we would identify its purpose and the question at issue, then examine the information provided, asking what each fact really shows, what assumptions are hidden and what else could explain the data. For example, if a clinic’s missed appointments rose after it moved, we would ask whether the move caused the change or whether other factors—new hours, staff changes or bus routes—also played a role, because correlation is not causation. We would separate facts from opinions and inferences and check numbers carefully, including denominators and time periods. Awareness of cognitive biases would protect our decisions. We would avoid anchoring on the first idea or number mentioned, guard against confirmation bias by looking for information that challenges our favorite idea, avoid the sunk cost fallacy if an idea we spent time on is not working and prevent groupthink by inviting every member to share concerns. Using a structured process engages slower, deliberate System 2 thinking instead of relying on fast intuition. Before presenting, we would test our solution against intellectual standards—clarity, accuracy, relevance, depth and fairness—and after practice rounds we would debrief using metacognition to improve how we think as a team.'},sources:[RULES,FCT,OS_OB]};

export const CP3={title:'Components of logic',hook:'“All healthy diets include vegetables. This diet includes vegetables. Therefore, this diet is healthy.” It sounds convincing—but it is logically invalid. Components of Logic make up 20% of the test, and logical reasoning also makes your Round Two solution more persuasive. This lesson covers arguments, deduction and induction, validity and soundness and common fallacies.',objectives:['Identify premises, conclusions and the structure of arguments.','Distinguish deductive, inductive and abductive reasoning.','Evaluate validity and soundness, including syllogisms and conditional statements.','Recognize common logical fallacies.'],sections:[
['Arguments, premises and conclusions',[
'In logic, an **argument** is a set of statements in which some (**premises**) are offered as reasons to accept another (the **conclusion**). Indicator words help: “because,” “since” and “given that” often introduce premises; “therefore,” “thus” and “so” often introduce conclusions.',
'To analyze an argument, **identify the conclusion first**, then find the premises and ask whether they support it. Watch for **unstated premises** (hidden assumptions) that the argument depends on.',
'Clear argument structure also strengthens presentations: state your conclusion (solution), then give the reasons and evidence.',
sort('Premise or conclusion?',['Premise','Conclusion'],[['Because missed appointments rose 30%…',0],['Therefore, the clinic should send text reminders.',1],['Since most patients own cell phones…',0],['So the reminder program is likely to work.',1],['Given that the budget is $5,000…',0]])]],
['Deductive reasoning',[
'**Deductive reasoning** moves from general premises to a specific conclusion that **must** be true if the premises are true. Example: “All mammals have lungs. Whales are mammals. Therefore, whales have lungs.” Deductive arguments are judged by **validity** and **soundness**.',
'An argument is **valid** if the conclusion follows necessarily from the premises—if the premises were true, the conclusion could not be false. It is **sound** if it is valid **and** its premises are actually true. A valid argument can have a false conclusion if a premise is false.',
'The opening example (“This diet includes vegetables, therefore it is healthy”) is **invalid**: including vegetables does not guarantee a diet is healthy. This error is called **affirming the consequent** in its conditional form.',
match('Term and meaning',[['Deductive reasoning','General premises lead to a certain conclusion'],['Valid','Conclusion follows necessarily from premises'],['Sound','Valid with true premises'],['Premise','A reason offered for a conclusion'],['Conclusion','The claim being supported'],['Unstated premise','A hidden assumption']])]],
['Syllogisms and conditional logic',[
'A **categorical syllogism** has two premises and a conclusion about categories: “All A are B; all B are C; therefore all A are C.” **Conditional (if–then) statements** have an **antecedent** (“if” part) and a **consequent** (“then” part). Two valid forms are **modus ponens** (If P, then Q; P; therefore Q) and **modus tollens** (If P, then Q; not Q; therefore not P).',
'Two invalid forms are **affirming the consequent** (If P, then Q; Q; therefore P) and **denying the antecedent** (If P, then Q; not P; therefore not Q). Example of affirming the consequent: “If a patient has the flu, they have a fever. This patient has a fever. Therefore, they have the flu.” Many conditions cause fever.',
'These forms appear frequently on logic tests; practice identifying them quickly.',
sort('Valid or invalid form?',['Valid','Invalid'],[['If P then Q; P; therefore Q (modus ponens)',0],['If P then Q; Q; therefore P',1],['If P then Q; not Q; therefore not P (modus tollens)',0],['If P then Q; not P; therefore not Q',1],['All A are B; all B are C; therefore all A are C',0]])]],
['Inductive and abductive reasoning',[
'**Inductive reasoning** moves from specific observations to a **probable** general conclusion: “Every patient surveyed preferred text reminders, so most patients probably prefer them.” Inductive arguments are judged as **strong** or **weak** based on sample size, representativeness and evidence; their conclusions can be wrong even when premises are true.',
'**Abductive reasoning** infers the **best explanation** for observations: “Attendance dropped right after the bus route changed, so the route change is the likely cause.” Clinicians use abduction in diagnosis. It is useful but tentative—other explanations must be considered.',
'In problem solving, induction helps you generalize from data and abduction helps you form hypotheses about causes; both should be tested.',
sort('Type of reasoning',['Deductive','Inductive','Abductive'],[['All licensed nurses passed an exam; Maya is a licensed nurse; Maya passed an exam',0],['Five clinics improved after reminders, so reminders probably help clinics',1],['Visits dropped after the route change, so the route change is the likely cause',2],['If it rains, the game is canceled; it rained; the game is canceled',0],['Most survey respondents prefer evening hours, so the community likely does',1]])]],
['Logical fallacies',[
'**Fallacies** are errors in reasoning. **Ad hominem**: attacking the person instead of the argument. **Straw man**: misrepresenting an argument to attack it easily. **False dilemma**: presenting only two options when more exist. **Slippery slope**: claiming one step will inevitably lead to extreme consequences. **Hasty generalization**: concluding from too little evidence. **Appeal to authority**: accepting a claim only because an authority said it (especially outside their expertise). **Circular reasoning**: using the conclusion as a premise. **Post hoc**: assuming that because B followed A, A caused B. **Bandwagon**: believing something because many people do.',
'Recognizing fallacies helps on the test and protects your team from weak reasoning during prep.',
'In your presentation, avoid fallacies such as overstating causes or dismissing alternatives without reasons.',
match('Fallacy and example',[['Post hoc','Scores rose after the program, so the program caused it'],['False dilemma','Either we close the clinic or go bankrupt'],['Bandwagon','Everyone uses this app, so it must work'],['Circular reasoning','This plan is best because it is the best plan'],['Hasty generalization','Two students liked it, so all will'],['Appeal to authority','A celebrity says it is healthy, so it is']])]],
['Logic in solutions and presentations',[
'Strong solutions follow a logical chain: **problem → causes → criteria → options → choice → plan → expected results**. Each step should follow from the one before. If you claim your solution will reduce missed appointments, explain the **mechanism** (why it works) and the **evidence** (data provided or prior experience).',
'Use clear logical connectors in your presentation: “Because the data show…, we chose…, which will…” Judges score **explanation of solution** on whether it is clear, concise and logical, and **understanding** on whether you grasp the problem’s causes.',
'Test your own logic: Would the conclusion still hold if one premise were false? Have we assumed something unproven?',
ex('A logical solution chain','Build a logic chain for an invented problem: low teen volunteer numbers at a hospital.',['Problem: teen volunteers fell from 60 to 30 in a year (data provided)','Cause: survey shows volunteer shifts conflict with sports practices','Criteria: low cost, fits a $3,000 budget, starts within a month','Option chosen: flexible weekend micro-shifts and a sign-up app','Mechanism: shifts fit teens’ schedules, removing the main barrier','Expected result: return to 50 volunteers within six months'],'Each step follows from the evidence and leads logically to the expected result (invented for practice).')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Spotting a flaw',{a:{text:'A teammate argues, “If our solution is good, judges will like it. Judges liked it in practice, so our solution is good.” (Scenario invented for practice.) What is wrong?',choices:[['Affirming the consequent—judges might like it for other reasons','b','Correct.',true],['Nothing; it is valid','b','This form is invalid.',false]]},b:{text:'Another says, “Either we build a new clinic or nothing will improve.” Which fallacy?',choices:[['False dilemma','c','Correct. Many other options exist.',true],['Ad hominem','c','No person is attacked.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team lists several realistic options before choosing.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Arguments have premises (reasons) and a conclusion; identify the conclusion first and watch for unstated premises; indicator words such as “because” and “therefore” help.','Deduction gives certain conclusions—valid if the conclusion follows, sound if also true; modus ponens and modus tollens are valid, while affirming the consequent and denying the antecedent are invalid; induction gives probable conclusions and abduction infers the best explanation.','Recognize fallacies (ad hominem, straw man, false dilemma, slippery slope, hasty generalization, appeal to authority, circular reasoning, post hoc, bandwagon) and build solutions as a logical chain from problem to expected result.'],terms:[['Valid argument','An argument whose conclusion must be true if its premises are true.'],['Sound argument','A valid argument with true premises.'],['Modus tollens','If P then Q; not Q; therefore not P.']],mcqs:[q('An argument is sound when it is:','Valid and has true premises','Popular','Long','Inductive','Both conditions.'),q('If P then Q; P; therefore Q is:','Modus ponens','Modus tollens','Affirming the consequent','Denying the antecedent','Valid form.'),q('If P then Q; Q; therefore P is:','Affirming the consequent','Modus ponens','Modus tollens','A syllogism','Invalid form.'),q('Inductive reasoning produces conclusions that are:','Probable','Certain','Always false','Circular','Specific to general.'),q('Inferring the best explanation for observations is:','Abductive reasoning','Deductive reasoning','Circular reasoning','A straw man','Diagnosis-like.'),q('“Scores rose after the program, so the program caused it” is:','Post hoc','Bandwagon','Ad hominem','Circular reasoning','After, therefore because of.'),q('Using the conclusion as a premise is:','Circular reasoning','Hasty generalization','False dilemma','Modus ponens','Begging the question.'),q('Which word usually introduces a conclusion?','Therefore','Because','Since','Given that','Indicator word.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the difference between deductive, inductive and abductive reasoning, give an example of each related to a health problem and describe how logical reasoning strengthens a Round Two solution.',rubric:[['Deductive',['deductive','general','certain','valid','sound','premises']],['Inductive and abductive',['inductive','probable','sample','abductive','best explanation','hypothesis']],['Examples',['example','clinic','patients','health','data']],['Application',['logic chain','causes','mechanism','evidence','fallacy','explanation']]],model:'Deductive reasoning moves from general premises to a conclusion that must be true if the premises are true: “All patients receiving this vaccine need a follow-up dose; Jordan received this vaccine; therefore Jordan needs a follow-up dose.” Such an argument is valid when the conclusion follows necessarily and sound when its premises are also true. Inductive reasoning moves from specific observations to a probable general conclusion: “In our survey of 200 clinic patients, 85% said text reminders would help them keep appointments, so most patients at the clinic probably prefer text reminders.” Its strength depends on the size and representativeness of the sample, and it could still be wrong. Abductive reasoning infers the best explanation for observations: “Missed appointments jumped right after the bus route stopped serving the clinic, so the route change is the most likely cause.” It generates hypotheses that need testing. Logical reasoning strengthens a Round Two solution by building a clear chain from problem to causes to criteria to options to the chosen solution and expected results. We would use the provided data inductively to describe the problem, use abduction to identify likely causes while considering alternatives, and explain deductively why our solution addresses those causes. We would also check our reasoning for fallacies, such as post hoc assumptions or false dilemmas, so judges see a clear, logical and convincing explanation.'},sources:[RULES,IEP_ARG,OWL_FALL]};

export const CP4={title:'Clarifying problems and finding root causes',hook:'Keyboards use the QWERTY layout not because it is the fastest, but because early typewriters jammed when neighboring keys were struck quickly—so the letters were spread out to slow typists down. Today the reason is gone, but the “invisible rule” remains. Many problems persist because no one questions how they are framed. Clarifying the problem is the first step to solving it well.',objectives:['Write a clear problem statement.','Distinguish symptoms from root causes.','Use the 5 Whys and fishbone diagrams to find causes.','Recognize and challenge invisible rules and assumptions.'],sections:[
['Why clarifying the problem matters',[
'Teams often rush to solutions and end up solving the **wrong problem**. A clear definition focuses effort, reveals causes and makes solutions more effective. The rubric’s first item—**understanding of the problem**—is worth 15 points, and judges notice when a solution addresses symptoms rather than causes.',
'Clarifying the problem means asking: What exactly is happening? Who is affected? Where and when does it occur? How big is it? What would success look like? What do we know, and what is uncertain?',
'Spend the first few minutes of Round Two prep on these questions; it saves time later.',
order('Questions that clarify a problem',['What exactly is happening?','Who is affected?','Where and when does it occur?','How large is the problem?','What would success look like?','What is uncertain?'])]],
['Writing a problem statement',[
'A **problem statement** describes the gap between the current situation and the desired situation, without proposing a solution. A useful format: “**[Who]** is experiencing **[what problem]**, shown by **[evidence]**, which causes **[impact]**.” For example: “Patients at Riverside Clinic missed 30% of appointments last year, up from 15%, delaying care and costing the clinic revenue.”',
'Avoid statements that assume a solution (“The clinic needs a new app”) or are too vague (“The clinic has problems”). A good statement makes the problem **specific**, **measurable** and **neutral**.',
'Write the problem statement at the top of your flip chart; it anchors your presentation.',
"Test your statement by asking whether a judge could understand the problem from it alone. If they would need more information, add the missing who, what or evidence.",
sort('Good problem statement?',['Good','Needs revision'],[['Missed appointments rose from 15% to 30% in one year at Riverside Clinic',0],['The clinic needs an app',1],['Teen volunteers fell from 60 to 30 this year, leaving weekend shifts unfilled',0],['Things are bad at the hospital',1],['Only 40% of members renewed HOSA dues this fall, down from 75%',0]])]],
['Symptoms versus root causes',[
'A **symptom** is a visible sign of a problem; a **root cause** is the underlying reason it happens. Treating symptoms brings temporary relief; addressing root causes prevents the problem from returning. In health care, **root cause analysis (RCA)** is used to investigate errors and adverse events and focuses on **systems and processes** rather than blaming individuals.',
'For example, long waiting room times (symptom) might result from overbooked schedules, too few staff at peak hours or patients arriving late because of transportation (possible root causes). Each root cause suggests a different solution.',
'Judges reward solutions that target root causes identified from the provided information.',
"Many problems have several interacting root causes. Prioritize the ones that are most supported by the data and most within your solution’s reach, and say so in your presentation.",
sort('Symptom or root cause?',['Symptom','Root cause'],[['Long waiting room times',0],['Schedules overbooked at peak hours',1],['Low volunteer numbers',0],['Shifts conflict with sports practices',1],['Students skipping breakfast',0],['Buses arrive too late for school breakfast service',1]])]],
['The 5 Whys',[
'The **5 Whys** technique asks “Why?” repeatedly—about five times—to move from a symptom to a root cause. Example: Patients miss appointments. **Why?** They forget. **Why?** Appointments are scheduled weeks ahead with no reminder. **Why?** The clinic stopped reminder calls. **Why?** The staff member who made calls left. **Why?** Reminder calls were never built into a system. Root cause: no reliable reminder system.',
'The 5 Whys is quick and works well during a 15-minute prep, but it can oversimplify problems with multiple causes. Use it alongside other tools and the data provided.',
'Write the chain briefly on index cards; it helps you explain your understanding to judges.',
"Stop asking “why” when you reach a cause you can act on. Going too far can lead to causes no solution can address, such as “people are busy.”",
order('5 Whys example',['Patients miss appointments','They forget','No reminders for appointments booked weeks ahead','Reminder calls stopped','The staff member who made calls left','Root cause: no reliable reminder system'])]],
['Fishbone diagrams',[
'A **fishbone (Ishikawa or cause-and-effect) diagram** organizes possible causes into categories branching from the problem, like bones from a fish’s spine. Common health care categories include **people**, **processes/methods**, **equipment/technology**, **environment**, **materials** and **policies**.',
'Fishbone diagrams help teams see **multiple causes** at once and avoid fixating on one. During prep, a quick fishbone sketch on scrap paper or an index card can organize your thinking; a clean version on a flip chart can show judges your understanding.',
'After brainstorming causes, circle the ones best supported by the provided data.',
match('Fishbone category and example cause',[['People','Not enough staff at peak hours'],['Processes','No reminder system'],['Technology','Outdated scheduling software'],['Environment','Clinic far from bus routes'],['Policies','No evening appointments'],['Materials','Reminder cards not printed']])]],
['Invisible rules and reframing',[
'In **Solve It!**, Dietmar Sternad describes **invisible rules**—unspoken assumptions about how things must be done, like the QWERTY keyboard, which was designed to **slow typing** and prevent jams on early typewriters. Many problems persist because people never question these rules: “Clinics must be open 9 to 5,” “Volunteers must work four-hour shifts,” “Health education must happen in a classroom.”',
'**Reframing** means looking at a problem from a different angle: instead of “How do we get more volunteers?” ask “How might we make volunteering fit teens’ lives?” Instead of “How do we reduce missed appointments?” ask “How might we bring care to patients who struggle to come in?” Reframing often unlocks creative solutions—and innovation points.',
'Ask: What are we assuming? Who says it must be this way? What if the opposite were true?',
"Sternad also encourages questioning the problem’s boundaries: Is the problem really about volunteers, or about the hospital’s staffing? Widening or narrowing the frame can reveal very different solutions.",
chk('Why were QWERTY keyboards designed with letters spread out?',['To slow down typing so early typewriters would not jam','To use power fingers for common letters','To use all fingers equally','To increase typing speed'],'An “invisible rule” that outlived its reason (Sternad).')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Reframing a problem',{a:{text:'An invented problem: only 40% of HOSA members renewed this year. The provided survey says many found meetings boring and at inconvenient times. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is a root cause?',choices:[['Meeting format and timing do not meet members’ needs','b','Correct.',true],['Members are lazy','b','Blaming people is not root cause analysis.',false]]},b:{text:'Which reframe opens more creative solutions?',choices:[['“How might we make HOSA engaging and convenient for busy students?”','c','Correct.',true],['“How do we force members to renew?”','c','That limits creativity.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team generates ideas like short hands-on skill nights and virtual check-ins.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Clarify before solving: what, who, where, when, how big and what success looks like; write a specific, measurable, neutral problem statement without a solution.','Distinguish symptoms from root causes; root cause analysis focuses on systems, not blame; use the 5 Whys and fishbone diagrams (people, processes, technology, environment, policies, materials).','Challenge invisible rules (QWERTY was designed to slow typing and prevent jams) and reframe with “How might we…?” questions to unlock creative, innovative solutions.'],terms:[['Problem statement','A neutral description of the gap between the current and desired situation.'],['Root cause','The underlying reason a problem occurs.'],['Invisible rule','An unspoken assumption about how things must be done.']],mcqs:[q('Invisible rules exist, such as keyboards designed with QWERTY as the first six letters. Why were keyboards created in this fashion?','Slows down the speed of typing as the letters are spread around the keyboard','Uses power fingers to type the most common letters','Maintains the equal use of all fingers','Increases the speed of typing by standardizing the keyboard','HOSA sample question (Sternad).'),q('Which is a good problem statement?','Missed appointments rose from 15% to 30% in one year at Riverside Clinic','The clinic needs an app','Things are bad','Patients are irresponsible','Specific and neutral.'),q('A visible sign of a problem is a:','Symptom','Root cause','Solution','Constraint','Treat causes.'),q('The 5 Whys technique is used to:','Move from a symptom to a root cause','Generate a budget','Choose team roles','Write a charter','Repeated questioning.'),q('A fishbone diagram is also called:','An Ishikawa or cause-and-effect diagram','A decision matrix','A Gantt chart','A SWOT analysis','Categories of causes.'),q('Root cause analysis in health care focuses on:','Systems and processes rather than blame','Punishing individuals','Only equipment','Only costs','Safety culture.'),q('Which is a reframe?','“How might we make volunteering fit teens’ lives?”','“How do we force teens to volunteer?”','“Why are teens lazy?”','“Nothing can be done.”','Opens creativity.'),q('A problem statement should NOT:','Propose a solution','Be specific','Be measurable','Describe impact','Keep it neutral.')],frq:{prompt:'Using an invented health-related problem, write a problem statement, use the 5 Whys to find a root cause and explain how reframing could lead to a more creative solution.',rubric:[['Problem statement',['who','what','evidence','impact','specific','measurable','neutral']],['5 Whys',['why','root cause','chain','system']],['Symptoms vs causes',['symptom','root cause','fishbone','categories','data']],['Reframing',['how might we','assumption','invisible rule','creative','innovative']]],model:'Invented problem statement: “At Lakeview Middle School, the percentage of students eating breakfast before first period fell from 70% to 45% this year, as shown by the school nutrition survey, contributing to more students visiting the nurse with headaches and fatigue.” Using the 5 Whys: Why are fewer students eating breakfast? Many do not eat at home. Why? Families leave early for work. Why don’t they eat at school? The cafeteria breakfast line closes before many buses arrive. Why? Two bus routes were changed this fall and now arrive ten minutes later. Why wasn’t breakfast adjusted? The breakfast schedule is set by a long-standing rule that service ends when first period begins. The root cause is a mismatch between bus arrival times and a fixed breakfast service window—a system problem, not a student problem. A fishbone diagram would also show other contributing causes, such as limited menu choices, but the data point most strongly to timing. Reframing the problem from “How do we get students to the cafeteria earlier?” to “How might we bring breakfast to students when and where they are?” challenges the invisible rule that breakfast must be served in the cafeteria before class. That reframe opens creative solutions such as grab-and-go breakfast carts at bus drop-off points or “breakfast in the classroom” during homeroom, which address the root cause directly and are likely to be more effective and innovative.'},sources:[RULES,PSNET_RCA,ASQ_FISH]};

export const CP5={title:'Generating creative ideas',hook:'The first idea a team thinks of is usually the most obvious—and the one every other team will present. Judges award 15 points for an imaginative and innovative approach, missing only the “wow” factor at the second level. Creative idea-generation techniques help your team move past the obvious to solutions that are fresh, clever and still practical.',objectives:['Apply brainstorming rules and divergent thinking.','Use structured creativity techniques such as SCAMPER and mind mapping.','Use lateral thinking and analogies to find new ideas.','Move from many ideas to a few strong ones.'],sections:[
['Creativity and innovation',[
'**Creativity** is producing ideas that are **novel** and **useful**. **Innovation** is putting creative ideas into practice. Organizational behavior research emphasizes that creativity depends on **expertise**, **creative thinking skills** and **motivation**, and that environments that encourage risk-taking, time to explore and diverse perspectives produce more creative ideas.',
'Creativity is not just artistic talent—it is a skill that improves with techniques and practice. Teams that practice idea-generation methods produce more and better ideas under pressure.',
'In Round Two, creativity must be paired with feasibility: judges want innovative ideas that could really work within constraints.',
"Organizational behavior research also shows that creativity grows when people have enough time to incubate ideas, freedom to experiment and leaders who treat mistakes as learning. In a competition setting, you create those conditions by practicing often, welcoming unusual suggestions and reviewing what worked after each practice problem.",
sort('Creative and useful?',['Creative and useful','Not both'],[['Grab-and-go breakfast carts at bus drop-off',0],['Hiring a celebrity chef for every school',1],['Text-message appointment reminders with a one-tap reschedule link',0],['Telling patients to try harder',1],['Peer “health ambassadors” leading short skill demos',0]])]],
['Brainstorming rules',[
'Classic **brainstorming** follows four rules: **defer judgment** (no criticism during idea generation), **go for quantity** (more ideas increase the chance of good ones), **encourage wild ideas** (they can be tamed later) and **build on others’ ideas** (“yes, and…”). Set a short time limit and have one person record every idea.',
'Brainstorming can fail if a few people dominate or members fear judgment. **Brainwriting**—members silently write ideas on cards before sharing—helps quieter members contribute and reduces groupthink. This works well with the index cards allowed in prep.',
'In Round Two, two to three minutes of focused brainstorming can produce 15–20 ideas to choose from.',
"A useful rule of thumb is to aim for at least ten ideas before discussing any of them. The first few are usually obvious; the later ones are often more original.",
order('Quick brainstorm in prep',['Restate the problem as “How might we…?”','Silently write ideas on cards for one minute','Share ideas without criticism','Build on each other’s ideas','Group similar ideas','Choose the most promising'])]],
['SCAMPER and structured techniques',[
'**SCAMPER** prompts new ideas by changing an existing approach: **Substitute** (replace a part), **Combine** (merge ideas), **Adapt** (borrow from elsewhere), **Modify/Magnify/Minify** (change size or form), **Put to another use**, **Eliminate** (remove a part) and **Reverse/Rearrange** (change order or flip roles).',
'Example for missed appointments: **Substitute** phone calls with texts; **Combine** reminders with transportation vouchers; **Adapt** airline check-in apps; **Modify** appointment length; **Put to another use** the waiting room as a telehealth booth; **Eliminate** the need to travel with telehealth; **Reverse** roles by bringing mobile clinics to patients.',
'Structured prompts are especially useful when a team gets stuck.',
"Apply SCAMPER quickly by assigning each letter to a teammate during brainwriting. Within a minute, the team has seven different directions to consider.",
match('SCAMPER prompt and example',[['Substitute','Texts instead of phone calls'],['Combine','Reminders plus transportation vouchers'],['Adapt','Borrow airline check-in apps'],['Eliminate','Telehealth removes travel'],['Reverse','Mobile clinic comes to patients'],['Put to another use','Waiting room becomes a telehealth booth']])]],
['Lateral thinking and analogies',[
'**Lateral thinking**, a term from Edward de Bono, means approaching problems from unexpected directions rather than step-by-step logic alone. Techniques include **random entry** (connecting the problem to a random word), **provocation** (“What if patients scheduled their own appointments?”) and **challenging assumptions**.',
'**Analogies** borrow solutions from other fields: How do restaurants reduce no-shows (deposits, reminders)? How do airlines manage overbooking? How do video games keep players engaged? Borrowing from unrelated industries often produces the “wow” factor.',
'De Bono’s **Six Thinking Hats** also helps teams look at ideas from different perspectives: facts (white), feelings (red), cautions (black), benefits (yellow), creativity (green) and process (blue).',
"Analogies work best when you name the underlying principle. Restaurants reduce no-shows by confirming reservations a day ahead; the principle is a timely reminder with an easy way to cancel, which a clinic can borrow.",
flip('Six Thinking Hats',[['White','Facts and information'],['Red','Feelings and intuition'],['Black','Risks and cautions'],['Yellow','Benefits and optimism'],['Green','Creativity and new ideas'],['Blue','Managing the thinking process']])]],
['Mind mapping and design thinking',[
'A **mind map** places the problem in the center and branches out to causes, ideas and connections. It shows relationships visually and can become a clear flip-chart graphic.',
'**Design thinking**, popularized by IDEO, emphasizes **empathy** for the people affected, **defining** the problem from their perspective, **ideating** many options, **prototyping** simple versions and **testing**. Even in a 15-minute prep, asking “What would this feel like for the patient or student?” leads to more human-centered, creative solutions.',
'Mentioning a user’s perspective in your presentation shows judges deep understanding.',
"Empathy can be built into prep in seconds: imagine one specific person affected by the problem—a single parent working two jobs, a teen athlete, an older adult without a car—and ask what a day in their life looks like. Solutions designed for real people are more creative and more convincing.",
order('Design thinking stages',['Empathize with the people affected','Define the problem from their view','Ideate many solutions','Prototype a simple version','Test and refine'])]],
['From many ideas to a few strong ones',[
'After generating ideas, **converge**: group similar ideas, remove ones that clearly violate constraints and select a few to evaluate. Quick methods include **dot voting** (each member marks favorites), an **impact–effort matrix** (high impact, low effort first) and checking each idea against the rubric: Is it innovative? Feasible? Financially sound? Does it use the provided information?',
'Often the best solution **combines** several ideas into a package—for example, text reminders plus transportation vouchers plus evening hours. A combined solution can address multiple root causes and show creativity.',
'Leave enough prep time to plan the chosen solution in detail; generating ideas should take only a few minutes.',
sort('Impact–effort matrix',['Do first (high impact, low effort)','Consider carefully (high impact, high effort)','Probably skip (low impact)'],[['Text reminders using existing software',0],['Building a new clinic',1],['A slogan contest with no follow-up',2],['Evening hours one day a week',0],['A citywide transit overhaul',1],['Colored pens in the waiting room',2]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Breaking out of the obvious',{a:{text:'For an invented problem about low hand-washing in a school, every idea so far is “make more posters.” (Scenario invented for practice.) What helps most?',choices:[['Use SCAMPER and analogies to generate different kinds of ideas','b','Correct.',true],['Choose the poster idea and move on','b','Push past the obvious.',false]]},b:{text:'Your team generates a glow-in-the-dark germ demo, a sink “high score” game and peer ambassadors. How do you choose?',choices:[['Check impact, effort, cost and fit with the provided data, possibly combining ideas','c','Correct.',true],['Pick the funniest idea','c','Balance creativity with feasibility.',false]]},c:{text:'You present a combined, low-cost solution with a clear “wow” element.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Creativity produces novel and useful ideas; innovation puts them into practice; creativity is a skill built through techniques, practice and supportive environments.','Brainstorm by deferring judgment, going for quantity, welcoming wild ideas and building on others; use brainwriting, SCAMPER, lateral thinking, analogies, Six Thinking Hats, mind maps and design thinking’s empathy.','Converge with grouping, dot voting and an impact–effort matrix; check ideas against the rubric and constraints; combine ideas to address multiple root causes and add a “wow” factor.'],terms:[['SCAMPER','A technique that prompts ideas by substituting, combining, adapting, modifying, putting to other use, eliminating or reversing.'],['Lateral thinking','Approaching problems from unexpected directions.'],['Brainwriting','Writing ideas silently before sharing them.']],mcqs:[q('Which is a brainstorming rule?','Defer judgment','Criticize every idea','Stop after three ideas','Only the leader speaks','Quantity first.'),q('In SCAMPER, “E” stands for:','Eliminate','Examine','Evaluate','Explain','Remove a part.'),q('Which hat in Six Thinking Hats represents risks and cautions?','Black','Yellow','Green','White','Critical judgment.'),q('Borrowing a solution from another industry is using an:','Analogy','Anchor','Assumption','Audit','Lateral thinking.'),q('Design thinking begins with:','Empathizing with the people affected','Building a budget','Writing a charter','Choosing a slogan','Human-centered.'),q('An idea with high impact and low effort should be:','Done first','Skipped','Delayed indefinitely','Ignored','Impact–effort matrix.'),q('Brainwriting helps:','Quieter members contribute and reduces groupthink','Only the loudest member','Avoid ideas','Speed up criticism','Silent writing.'),q('Creativity is producing ideas that are:','Novel and useful','Expensive','Complicated','Popular only','Definition.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would generate and select creative ideas during the 15-minute Round Two prep for an invented health problem.',rubric:[['Divergent techniques',['brainstorm','defer judgment','quantity','brainwriting','scamper','analogy','lateral']],['Empathy and reframing',['how might we','empathy','user','design thinking','perspective']],['Convergence',['group','dot voting','impact','effort','constraints','rubric','combine']],['Time and outcome',['minutes','innovative','feasible','financially','wow']]],model:'For an invented problem about low flu vaccination among high school students, we would spend about three minutes generating ideas. First, we would restate the problem as “How might we make getting a flu shot easy and appealing for students?” and take one minute of silent brainwriting on index cards so every member contributes, then share ideas without criticism, building on each other’s suggestions. When ideas cluster around posters, we would use SCAMPER—substituting a school-based clinic for a doctor’s visit, combining the clinic with a sports physical day, adapting a “streak” feature from fitness apps, eliminating the need for parents to take time off with signed consent forms sent home in advance and reversing roles by having student athletes act as ambassadors. We would also borrow analogies, such as how concerts sell tickets with countdowns and limited spots. Thinking with empathy, we would ask what makes vaccination hard for a student—fear of needles, missing class and transportation—and add ideas that address those barriers. Then we would converge: grouping similar ideas, removing any that violate the provided budget and using quick dot voting and an impact–effort matrix to pick the strongest. We would likely combine a free school clinic during lunch, athlete ambassadors sharing short videos and a class competition with a small prize, creating an innovative, feasible and financially sound package that uses the data provided.'},sources:[RULES,IDEO,OS_OB]};
